From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Faust <willtechnoduck@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 01:14:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] iOS: implement 128-bit CASPAL, bound the callret leak, add
 CPU-area/dispatcher probes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

FORK-ONLY. Not for upstream (see FEX/CLAUDE.md); user approved fork edits 2026-07-29.

128-bit CASPAL (Arm64.cpp) — RunCASPAL had no Size==1 branch, so every 128-bit CAS
fell through to `return false` -> "Unhandled JIT SIGBUS CASPAL" -> fatal
STATUS_DATATYPE_MISALIGNMENT. That ended ml258 at unix_calls=24293 on
  CASPAL x6,x7, x4,x5, [x11]  (insn 0x4866fd64, misalign=0, crosses16B=no)
so it was never an alignment problem, just unimplemented. Added Size==1 mirroring the
Size==0 conventions: reg 31 reads as 0 (only the upper reg can be 31), misaligned
returns false unchanged (CASP with 64-bit operands requires 16-byte alignment and the
LOCK CMPXCHG16B it comes from #GPs when unaligned, so a correct guest cannot emit it),
no split-lock handling needed (a 16-byte aligned 16-byte access cannot cross a 64-byte
cacheline), and on failure the observed value is written back to the Rs pair so the
guest's retry loop converges. Verified: caspal128 1 -> 0.

callret leak (BranchOps.cpp, all 3 guard sites) — the >>24 test only fired when the
pointer left the ENTIRE 16MB region, so a large in-range leak sailed through. Measured
on the CEF webhelper: tid 0098 had 1.1MB pushed (~36,000 nested calls) and tid 0078
2.75MB (~90,000) while those threads' GUEST stacks had used only 2,264 and 10,008
bytes — impossible, since every x86 CALL pushes >=8 bytes. Non-local exits (SEH unwind,
C++ throw) skip the guest RETs and CEF init throws constantly. Now bounded to a 4MB
window centred on DefaultLocation. Safe by construction: this stack is a return-address
PREDICTOR, so a stale entry fails the RipReg compare and falls through to the L1 lookup.

Probes: [cpu-area] at ThreadInit + at the silent null-ThreadState bail (which returned
STATUS_SUCCESS with no trace), now including EnterEC so a faulting pool PC can be made
dispatcher-relative; [callret-geom] once per process (also the only greppable marker
for the emitter-side guard change); [ir-null] guard at GenerateIR.

Deliberately uses LogMan, not the WriteFile(hStdError) path the existing
"ThreadInit() done" line uses — that one has never once appeared in a log, so its
silence carries no information.

Madeira-commit: ceabf254afbdc3c4c943d238f8c3082a13595028
Rebased: clean
Class: madeira-port
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-09-24-fex-latest-rebase.md
Offered-upstream: no
